ac{2}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ |\n\nGiven the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ above for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ calculate $P(Y<2).$" :question/worked-solution "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{3}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{4}{7}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{2}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{3}{7}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $\\frac{5}{7}$ | $\\frac{2}{7}$ | $1$ |\n\nThe marginal mass function for $Y$ is given in the following table:\n\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{5}{7}$ | $\\frac{2}{7}$ |\n\nFinally, using the table of values of the marginal mass function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<2) & =P(Y\\in {1}) \\\\  & =P(Y=1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-142575/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96473ec4-67f8-5614-b187-0961062aac29"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-142575"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-142714/selection/95a1d5fa369c4d08bda10228102d0fbae8f93732afc4c2ec3773c4f3d47df28c" :answer/id #uuid "2864b101-6085-52d5-8378-6eadcf8d3b25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142714/selection/e459c513a8ab41d90fbedd18ffc02e71387b30263f9b62ed7d74cc4e1864eeb6" :answer/id #uuid "6d09c576-0bf0-5c4b-9e30-f7aa829ec996" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.55" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142714/selection/bd0c5293c05dbcd56341c67931e9e0ae46e20e1d0de3da140e1b2dcd87ea42e2" :answer/id #uuid "e6d458ad-38f6-5650-8ab7-4209de6e0e3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142714/selection/a44ed9268297050614edc4097a04d3089044bc70b376ad604b0445193a602b0a" :answer/id #uuid "6ad6495c-8275-51de-ae3f-cc887b29b2af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.65" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142714/selection/0e8c61e1cbe5b6d4b3e52c0431e48b853c5faa01ca7ca0f5f9cf780292b06b1f" :answer/id #uuid "835217a4-d6a2-5dd5-8659-84135a515386" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142714/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5958cea9-8fca-5086-acaf-2a49747a1e80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-142714/selection/e459c513a8ab41d90fbedd18ffc02e71387b30263f9b62ed7d74cc4e1864eeb6" :answer-field/choices ["q-142714/selection/95a1d5fa369c4d08bda10228102d0fbae8f93732afc4c2ec3773c4f3d47df28c" "q-142714/selection/e459c513a8ab41d90fbedd18ffc02e71387b30263f9b62ed7d74cc4e1864eeb6" "q-142714/selection/bd0c5293c05dbcd56341c67931e9e0ae46e20e1d0de3da140e1b2dcd87ea42e2" "q-142714/selection/a44ed9268297050614edc4097a04d3089044bc70b376ad604b0445193a602b0a" "q-142714/selection/0e8c61e1cbe5b6d4b3e52c0431e48b853c5faa01ca7ca0f5f9cf780292b06b1f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-142714" :question/id #uuid "70d19739-3637-5291-b9d5-a437e0e2cbed" :question/math-academy-id "q-142714" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ |\n\nGiven the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ above for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ calculate $P(Y\\le 1).$" :question/worked-solution "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.2$ | $0.55$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ | $0.45$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.25$ | $0.3$ | $0.45$ | $1$ |\n\nThe marginal mass function for $Y$ is given in the following table:\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.25$ | $0.3$ | $0.45$ |\n\nFinally, using the table of values of the marginal mass function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le 1) & =P(Y\\in {0,1}) \\\\  & =P(Y=0)+P(Y=1) \\\\  & =0.25+0.3 \\\\  & =0.55.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-142714/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96473ec4-67f8-5614-b187-0961062aac29"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-142714"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9189"] :question/problem "Given the joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ below for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ find $P(X = 0).$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |" :question/worked-solution "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{7}{12}}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ | $\\dfrac{7}{12}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, using the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0) & ={f}_{X}(0) \\\\  & =f(0,0)+f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{5}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-10044"] :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n\nGiven the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ above for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ find the marginal mass function for $X.$" :question/worked-solution "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.3}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.7}$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, the marginal mass function for $X$ is given by the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9190"] :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n\nGiven the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ above for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ calculate $P(X \\geq 2).$" :question/worked-solution "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.25}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.5}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.25}$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.35$ | $0.65$ | $1$ |\n\nThe marginal mass function for $X$ is given in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ |\n\nFinally, using the table of values of the marginal mass function for $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =P(X\\in {2,3}) \\\\  & =P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =0.5+0.25 \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
